'''''Rosa corymbifera''''', the '''thicket dog rose''', is a species of flowering plant in the family [[Rosaceae]].<ref name="ROSCF" >{{cite web |url=https://gd.eppo.int/taxon/ROSCF |title=''Rosa dumetorum'' (ROSCF) |author=<!--Not stated--> |date=2023 |website=EPPO Global Database |publisher=European and Mediterranean Plant Protection Organization |access-date=20 July 2023 }}</ref> It is native to northwestern Africa, nearly all of Europe, the Caucasus, the Middle East, and Central Asia.<ref name="POWO_732177-1" >{{cite POWO |id=732177-1 |title=''Rosa corymbifera'' Borkh. |access-date=20 July 2023 }}</ref> It primarily differs from the dog rose ''[[Rosa canina]]'' in having downier leaves, particularly on the abaxial surfaces (the undersides).<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.treesandshrubsonline.org/articles/rosa/rosa-canina/#11294 |title=''Rosa canina'' L. |author=<!--Not stated--> |date=2023 |website=Trees and Shrubs Online |publisher=International Dendrology Society |access-date=20 July 2023 }}</ref> The most widely used rootstock in Europe for garden roses is its cultivar 'Laxa'.<ref name="1_of_1310_Google_Scholar_results" >{{cite journal |title=Diversity of the genus ''Rosa'' pomological traits in ecological conditions of continental Croatia |year=2022 |last1=Tomljenovic |first1=Nikola |last2=Jemric |first2=Tomislav |last3=Vukovic |first3=Marko |journal=Genetika |volume=54 |issue=2 |pages=689–704 |doi=10.2298/GENSR2202689T |s2cid=253192258 |doi-access=free }}</ref>
